  "tell_me": "Based on 23 scraped states, 18 seed-data states, 3 states with non-percentage structures (unquantified), and 2 waiver-risk states, this analysis applies a three-tier model to assess how the OBBBA provider-tax provisions—which phase the allowable subject-class rate ceiling from 6% down to 3.5% between 2028 and 2032—affect state Medicaid financing across 51 jurisdictions.

TIER 1 (BUDGET DEPENDENCE): The tier-1 ranked list covers all 51 jurisdictions for which a computable budget-dependence share is available or confirmed. Among those with nonzero computable shares, the highest dependence is Rhode Island at 40.0% of estimated nonfederal Medicaid spending (rate_model_kff_anchor; scraped), followed by Louisiana at 21.2% (rate_model_kff_anchor; scraped) and Nevada at 23.4% (rate_model_kff_anchor; scraped). Note that all dependence figures carry the label 'rate_model_kff_anchor,' meaning they are model estimates calibrated to a KFF-reported 18% national median, not directly reported Title XIX expenditure data; they require T-19 verification before citation as reported figures.

TIER 2 (RATE MECHANISM): Of the 51 jurisdictions, 26 expansion-state jurisdictions have subject provider-tax rates above the 3.5% floor and are classified as exposed. These states face a phased revenue gap totaling $51.49B by 2032 if no policy changes are made. The top three exposed states by cumulative gap are: California ($12,510.8M; hospital tax at 5.03%; scraped), New York ($6,078.6M; hospital tax at 4.5%; scraped), and Illinois ($3,865.8M; hospital tax at 5.9%; scraped). The gap is entirely driven by subject provider-tax classes (hospital and MCO); nursing facility and ICF/IID taxes are exempt classes and are excluded from gap calculations. The phase-down schedule is: 5.5% ceiling in 2028, 5.0% in 2029, 4.5% in 2030, 4.0% in 2031, and 3.5% in 2032. IMPORTANT STATE-SPECIFIC NOTES: Pennsylvania (PA) has a hospital tax rate of 3.32% per a FY2022-23 PA Bulletin source, which is below the 3.5% floor; PA is therefore classified as non-exposed. However, this rate is sourced from a prior-year bulletin and must be verified against the current-year bulletin before PA's non-exposed status is cited. Kentucky's (KY) $980.2M cumulative gap runs entirely through its MCO tax (5.5%), not its hospital tax (2.5%); the adjustment pathway for KY is MCO contract renegotiation, not hospital-tax legislation. KY's MCO tax rate should be confirmed via the relevant CMS State Plan Amendment before the gap figure is cited.

TIER 3 (STRUCTURAL OUTLIERS): Separately, Rhode Island (RI; hospital tax 13.12%; scraped) and Nevada (NV; hospital tax 7.139%; scraped) operate above the 6% ceiling under CMS non-uniformity waivers. Under OBBBA, tightened uniformity requirements place their waiver status—not the 3.5% phase-down—as the primary compliance risk. Their dollar gap is recorded as $0.0M in the model because the applicable mechanism is waiver revocation, not rate phase-down; that $0.0 figure does not mean there is no financial exposure, only that the standard gap model does not apply. The following states have confirmed provider taxes in structures not directly comparable to the 6%→3.5% cap: Idaho (hospital: formula—rate × base = UPL payment, plus 30% GF add-on from July 2023; seed data), Montana (hospital: $70 per inpatient bed day plus 0.90% outpatient revenue; seed data), and Oregon (hospital: ≤30% Medicare FFS inpatient plus ≤41% Medicare FFS outpatient per OHA Director rule; seed data). Their rate-based exposure is not quantified;

